use @piton/belay
from ../Factory import Factory
from ./lib/FactorySkillBase import FactorySkillBase

export skill Factory0010Optimize extends FactorySkillBase:
    description: Assess factory bottlenecks and improve resource policy and model routing within engineer-approved limits.
    useWhen: tuning factory resource use, researching model options, or diagnosing throughput and cost problems
    prompt:
        Read @{Factory.resourceOptimization}, @{Factory.adaptiveModelSelection},
        @{Factory.accountAllocation}, and @{Factory.paidFallbackDecision}.
        Inspect the actual approved policy and telemetry for accounts, reset
        windows, API budgets, throttling, task queues, builds, reviews, merges,
        and completed outcomes. Separate observations from estimates.

        Diagnose the current bottleneck and propose changes that improve
        useful throughput within speed, quality, and economy constraints.
        Model suitability is task-specific; evaluate total time and cost to
        acceptance, including higher-tier review and correction effort, not
        just the first attempt. Avoid treating historical model rankings or
        advertised prices as permanent truths. Research current provider
        information from authoritative sources and retain dates and uncertainty.

        Routine control is deterministic code using the specified PID-style
        feedback approach. Help implement or tune it only within the task's
        scope; do not act as the recurring scheduler yourself or invent exact
        control variables and gains as settled requirements. Use approved
        accounts automatically within thresholds. If subscription capacity is
        the bottleneck, optimize within its allowance; if builds constrain
        throughput, align useful planning with build pace.

        Missing fresh usage data permits cautious estimates, not unlimited
        usage. Enforce budgets and allowance constraints. Ask before trying a
        newly researched model, adding funds, or choosing paid API fallback to
        avoid subscription throttling unless that policy is already approved.
        Keep unrelated work moving. Jev remains a candidate, not a required
        dependency or substitute for deterministic capacity enforcement.
    result:
        Report the bottleneck, evidence quality, measured outcome costs,
        proposed or authorized changes, and decisions needing engineer input.
